from c8e_base import *
LB(8,4,'Language work: odd word out, taste words, antonyms, past tenses, punctuation, syllables and a magazine article','Language work for The Cherry Tree: word groups, antonyms, past tenses, punctuation and an article','Growing words from a cherry tree',
 "Rakesh planted a cherry seed and watched it grow into a tree. The language work of this story teaches us to sort words, to describe tastes, to use opposites, to tell the story in the right past tense, to punctuate correctly and to write a magazine article.",
 [("Words in a list are often related, and one word may not fit. In 'chop, trim, slice, mince', the words chop, slice and mince are about cutting food with a knife, but trim means cutting a small part off with scissors. So trim is the odd word.","odd word out"),
  ("To find the odd word, first name what most words share, then check each one against it. In 'stared, peered, pecked, gazed', stared, peered and gazed are all ways of looking, while pecked is what a bird does with its beak.","stared, peered, gazed; pecked"),
  ("In 'growing, blossoming, progressing, planting', the first three mean developing, but planting means putting seeds or plants into the ground. Always give a reason, not just the word.","growing, planting"),
  ("Taste words let us describe food exactly. Sweet is like jaggery, sour is like lemon, bitter is like neem leaves, tangy is a sharp and fresh taste like tamarind, spicy is hot like a pickle, salty has salt, and bland means tasteless, like plain boiled vegetables or paneer.","sweet, sour, bitter, tangy, spicy, salty, bland"),
  ("Antonyms are words with opposite meanings. The text gives these pairs: tender and hard, sturdy and shaky, narrow and wide, warmth and coldness, feasting and fasting, grumpy and cheerful, sheltered and unprotected, special and ordinary.","tender/hard, sturdy/shaky, narrow/wide, warmth/coldness"),
  ("Tenses tell us when something happens. The simple past tense tells a finished action, as in 'Rakesh bought the cherries'. We use the second form of the verb, or the verb with -ed.","simple past tense"),
  ("The past progressive tense shows an action that was going on at a past time, and it is made with was or were and the -ing form: 'Rakesh was looking for Grandfather'. It also shows two actions together: 'I was listening while she was talking'.","past progressive tense"),
  ("The past perfect tense shows an action that was completed before another past action. It is made with had and the third form of the verb: 'Rakesh and Grandfather gazed at the tree as though it had performed a miracle'. The miracle happened before the gazing.","past perfect tense"),
  ("A good rule: use had plus the third form for the earlier action and the simple past for the later one. 'By the time I left, the sun had set' means the sun set first and then I left.","earlier action: had + past participle"),
  ("Punctuation marks are symbols that make writing clear. A full stop ends a statement, a comma separates items in a list or parts of a sentence, a question mark ends a direct question and an exclamation mark shows strong feeling.","full stop, comma, question mark, exclamation mark"),
  ("A colon introduces a list or an explanation, as in 'He had one goal: win the race'. A semicolon joins two closely related sentences, as in 'She likes reading; he prefers writing'. A dash adds emphasis, as in 'He was late, again'.","colon, semicolon, dash"),
  ("An apostrophe shows a contraction, as in he's for he is, and it shows ownership, as in Jagan's book and the girls' toys. If a singular noun already ends in s, we may add only an apostrophe, as in Manas' car. Quotation marks enclose the exact words someone says.","apostrophe, quotation marks"),
  ("Capital letters are used for names of people and places, days, months and festivals, titles with names such as Dr Sneha, deities and holy books, the pronoun I, adjectives made from proper nouns such as Indian, geographical features such as the Himalayas, languages and religions, and titles of books and poems.","capital letters"),
  ("A syllable is a single unbroken vowel sound in a word, and you can clap it out as a beat. Tree has one syllable, cherry has two, grandfather has three and caterpillar has four. Count the beats while you say the word aloud.","syllables"),
  ("For the listening task, you hear a mother and son talk about tree-saving movements such as Chipko and Appiko and about Jadav Payeng and Saalumarada Thimakka. Read the seven statements first and tick the four that match what they say.","listening task"),
  ("An article for a school magazine has a heading, the writer's name as a byline, and four paragraphs. Paragraph one introduces the topic, paragraph two gives facts and advantages, paragraph three gives consequences and disadvantages, and paragraph four gives suggestions and ends with hope, caution or a call for action.","article layout"),
  ("Linking words such as Moreover, Additionally and To conclude connect the paragraphs of an article. Moreover and Additionally add one more point, and To conclude signals the ending.","linking words")],
 ("Choose the odd word out and give a reason: 'sweet, sour, spicy, planting'. Then write one sentence in the past perfect tense.",
  [("Planting is the odd word, because sweet, sour and spicy are taste words.","Name what most words share."),
   ("Past perfect sentence: 'The tree had grown tall before we noticed it.'","Use had plus the third form of the verb.")]),
 ("Explain the difference between the past progressive tense and the past perfect tense with one example each.",
  ["past progressive shows an action that was going on, made with was or were plus -ing","past perfect shows an action finished before another past action, made with had plus the third form","an example of each in one sentence"],
  ["Using was with the third form of the verb","Using had for the later action instead of the earlier one"]),
 ["Odd word out: find what most words share and name the one that does not fit.","Taste words: sweet, sour, bitter, tangy, spicy, salty, bland.","Simple past for finished actions, past progressive (was/were + -ing) for actions in progress, past perfect (had + third form) for the earlier action.","Punctuation and capital letters make writing clear.","A magazine article has a heading, byline and four paragraphs."],
 [mcq("v4a-1",1,"In 'chop, trim, slice, mince', the odd word is","trim",[("chop","Chop is cutting with a knife."),("mince","Mince is cutting very fine with a knife.")],"Trim uses scissors to cut a part off."),
  mcq("v4a-2",1,"In 'stared, peered, pecked, gazed', the odd word is","pecked",[("stared","Stared is a way of looking."),("gazed","Gazed is a way of looking.")],"Pecked is a bird's action."),
  mcq("v4a-3",1,"Which taste word suits lemon?","sour",[("sweet","Sweet is the taste of jaggery."),("bland","Bland means tasteless.")],"Lemon is sour."),
  mcq("v4a-4",1,"The antonym of 'warmth' is","coldness",[("fasting","Fasting is the opposite of feasting."),("narrow","Narrow is the opposite of wide.")],"Warmth and coldness are opposites."),
  mcq("v4a-5",1,"The antonym of 'feasting' is","fasting",[("fussing","This is not its opposite."),("growing","This is not its opposite.")],"Feasting is eating a lot, fasting is not eating."),
  mcq("v4a-6",2,"'Rakesh was looking for Grandfather' is in the","past progressive tense",[("simple past tense","Simple past would be 'Rakesh looked'."),("past perfect tense","Past perfect would use 'had looked'.")],"Was plus -ing shows an action going on."),
  mcq("v4a-7",2,"Choose the past perfect sentence.","By the time we arrived, the show had ended.",[("We were arriving when the show ended.","This has two actions in progress or finishing together."),("We arrive after the show ends.","This is present tense.")],"Had ended is the earlier action."),
  mcq("v4a-8",2,"Fill in: 'By the time I left, the sun ___ (set).'","had set",[("was setting","This shows the sun still going down."),("sets","This is present tense.")],"The sun set before I left."),
  mcq("v4a-9",2,"Which mark introduces a list or an explanation?","colon",[("semicolon","It joins two related sentences."),("apostrophe","It shows ownership or a contraction.")],"A colon introduces."),
  mcq("v4a-10",2,"Correct form to show that the toys belong to several girls","the girls' toys",[("the girl's toys","This shows one girl."),("the girls toys","The apostrophe is missing.")],"Plural nouns ending in s take only an apostrophe."),
  num("v4a-11",2,"How many syllables are in the word 'caterpillar'?",4,"Cat-er-pil-lar has four beats.",[(3,"You missed one beat; clap it out slowly."),(2,"That is cherry.")]),
  mcq("v4a-12",3,"Which is correctly capitalised?","On Monday, Dr Sneha visited the Himalayas.",[("on monday, dr sneha visited the himalayas.","Days, titles with names and geographical names need capitals."),("On monday, Dr sneha visited the himalayas.","Some capitals are missing.")],"Days, titles with names and mountain ranges are capitalised."),
  mcq("v4a-13",3,"In a magazine article, which paragraph gives suggestions and a call for action?","the last paragraph",[("the first paragraph","That introduces the topic."),("the second paragraph","That gives facts and advantages.")],"Paragraph four ends with suggestions.")])
